<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.9.3: Understanding the Meaning of an Inequality</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00521</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.9.3: Understanding the Meaning of an Inequality</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1c85601a-a5fa-43ea-b3db-6897a7b4160d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>For questions 1 – 3, use the information about the orchards to answer each question.</para>
      <para>
        <quote>A teacher is choosing between two options for a class field trip to an orchard. At each orchard, the same price applies to both chaperones and students.</quote>
     </para>
      <!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="cbd28ee1-7fa0-4474-b9f7-6d4dc621303b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which orchard would be cheaper to visit if the class has 8 students?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Orchard B.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is Orchard B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
      <!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_5810148a-d726-4ca6-bd3f-1a616d5b2134" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which orchard would be cheaper to visit if the class has 12 students?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Orchard B.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is Orchard B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
      <!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_7ab20181-2576-4245-91a9-4db512aeb97f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which orchard would be cheaper to visit if the class has 30 students?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Orchard A.</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is Orchard A.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
      <para>Use the following information to answer questions 4 – 7.</para>
      <para>
        <quote>To help her compare the cost of her two options, the teacher first writes the equation \( 9(n + 3) = 10(n + 1) \), and then she writes the inequality \( 9(n + 3) &lt; 10(n + 1) \). 
  </quote>
     </para>
      <!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does \(n\) represent in each statement?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( n \) represents the number of students on the field trip.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>In this situation, what does the equation \( 9(n + 3) = 10(n + 1) \) mean?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( 9(n + 3) \) is the cost of going to Orchard A, and \( 10(n + 1) \) is the cost of going to Orchard B. The equation represents the two options costing the same amount.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does the solution to the inequality \( 9(n + 3) &lt; 10(n + 1) \) tell us?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The solution represents the number of students on the field trip at which it would be cheaper to visit Orchard A.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <!--Q#-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>The teacher needs a visual aid to show the school budgeting committee. Graph the solution set to the inequality on the number line. Be prepared to show or explain your reasoning.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\( n&gt;17 \)
    <figure><media alt="Inequality on a number line."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_06_01.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
      <title>Video: Understanding the Meaning of an Inequality </title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about the meaning of an inequality.</para>
